+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded of a pickup, or proposes sending reminders by email to save the SMS
+cost. The day before pickup, larder texts the member that their pantry order is ready and when to collect it. It
+goes by SMS, about 40 euros a month, because the trial of email reminders in 2025 more than doubled missed pickups.
+
+## Message
+
+The day before the pickup slot, `remind` in `larder/reminders.py` sends the member "Your pantry order is ready for
+pickup" followed by the slot. `CHANNEL` selects SMS, to the member's phone, or email, to their address; it is set to
+SMS.
+
+## Why SMS, not email
+
+Email is free, and volunteers ask to switch every year; [issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md) is the third request of
+its year. Larder already tried it: from April to June 2025 reminders went by email. The email provider's report showed
+34% of reminder emails never opened, and missed pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13%. Each missed pickup is
+a packed bag, and its fresh food goes in the bin at closing time. Many members share one phone in the family and never
+check email, but they see a text. Reminders went back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by
+August.
+
+A proposal to change the channel has to answer those numbers: a channel that reaches fewer members costs more in
+wasted food than it saves in SMS fees.
